{
    "title": "Review: Implicit, Explicit & Parametric",
    "pathToSources": "Courses/MathForComputerGraphics/Chapter 1/Block 0/",
    "steps": [
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Let’s review three ways to describe a set of points: implicit, explicit, and parametric.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "First, an implicit representation gives a condition. A point belongs to the set if it satisfies this condition.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Select all implicit equations:",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "x² + y² = 1",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "This is an implicit equation of a unit circle."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "y = x + 3",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "This is an explicit equation."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "x + y² - 2 = 0",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "This is an implicit equation of a parabola."
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Next, an explicit representation gives a formula that directly expresses one coordinate in terms of the other.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Select all explicit non-parametric equations:",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "x ∈ ℝ, y = x³",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "This is an explicit equation of a cubic function."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "x² + y² = 9",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "This is an implicit equation."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "x = sin(t), y = t",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "This is a parametric form."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "y = x² - 4",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "This is an explicit equation of a parabola."
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Finally, a parametric representation introduces a parameter, like t, and expresses each coordinate as a function of that parameter.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Select all parametric representations:",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "x = t², y = t³",
                        "isCorrect": true
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "x = cos(t), y = sin(t)",
                        "isCorrect": true
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "x = t, y = 1/t",
                        "isCorrect": true
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Compare these two sets: A = {[x, y] | x = t, y = t + 1} \nB = {[x, y] | y = x + 1}. What do they describe?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "The same line",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Correct! Both describe the same line."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "Two different curves",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "Not quite. They look different, but they define the same set of points."
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "The difference is in how the points are described: parametric form uses a parameter to generate them, while explicit form gives y directly in terms of x.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Can you always convert a parametric form into an explicit one easily?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "Yes",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "Not always! Consider a circle."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "No",
                        "isCorrect": true
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Let's think about a circle. There's no simple way to express y purely as a function of x.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "The best you can do is x² + y² = 1, which is implicit, not explicit!",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Match the description to the type of set representation:",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Pairing",
            "pairs": [
                {
                    "id": 0,
                    "left": "Condition points must fulfill",
                    "right": "Implicit"
                },
                {
                    "id": 1,
                    "left": "Instructions for generating points",
                    "right": "Explicit (Parametric)"
                },
                {
                    "id": 2,
                    "left": "Both x and y depend on a parameter",
                    "right": "Parametric"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Let's wrap up with a simple question: explicit, implicit, or parametric?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Flashcards",
            "flashcards": [
                {
                    "frontText": "y = x + 2",
                    "backText": "explicit"
                },
                {
                    "frontText": "x = t, y = t + 2",
                    "backText": "parametric"
                },
                {
                    "frontText": "x = cos(t), y = sin(t)",
                    "backText": "parametric"
                },
                {
                    "frontText": "x² + y = 6",
                    "backText": "implicit"
                },
                {
                    "frontText": "x + y² - 2 = 0",
                    "backText": "implicit"
                },
                {
                    "frontText": "y < x + 2",
                    "backText": "explicit"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Perfect, we're at the end. So what did we learn today?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "The differences between the representations",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Yes! Implicit, explicit, and parametric each have unique traits."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "The best formula for each set",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "Not quite. There isn't a 'best' formula; it depends on the context."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "Implicit — condition to check vs. Explicit — how to generate points",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Exactly! Implicit gives a condition, explicit tells how to get the points or lists them directly."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "To compare different representations of the same set",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Exactly! Different forms can describe the same set of points."
                    }
                ]
            }
        }
    ]
}